# S3-F: exact support at ordinary dual value updates

## Decision and scope

The original S3 roadmap includes value-update consumers independently of the
stopped DSE mixed-route investigation. This source/design step examines only
`update_duals!` and `update_primals!` in `src/dual_simplex.jl`, their ordinary
call site, and existing scratch support. It introduces no production candidate,
Julia execution, test execution, new LP, or numerical allowance.

**A general support-only replacement is not admitted.** The dual loop repairs
costs outside support. Floating primal updates can change signed zero even when
the direction coefficient is zero. The next bounded source-only question is
whether the exact `Rational{BigInt}` primal consumer has a usable, inexpensive
admission contract; it is not yet accepted for implementation. This does not
reopen DSE, HH, controller accounting, loading isolation, or S2-E tuning.

## Consumer semantics

`update_duals!` (lines 566–591) visits all N reduced costs, skipping basic entries
and the entering index. Each remaining entry subtracts the dual-step product.
For a nonfixed variable it also checks the current bound state and tolerance,
shifts a violating working cost to restore a zero reduced cost, marks
perturbation, and invalidates the pricing pool when costs changed. Leaving and
entering reduced costs are assigned separately after the loop.

A zero tableau coefficient does not remove that repair obligation. The existing
`Dual working-cost shifts invalidate cached candidates` test in
`test/partial_pricing_integration_tests.jl` supplies an all-zero tableau row and
zero step, yet expects a working-cost change and pricing-pool invalidation.
`repair_cost_and_observe!` in `test/dual_stagnation_cost_tests.jl` exercises the
same zero-row repair for multiple scalar types. These are source-read test
contracts, not new execution evidence. No omission of the N-entry loop is
proposed here.

`update_primals!` (lines 593–600) visits m basic indices in basis-row order,
performs `primal[index] -= primal_step * tableau_column[row]`, then adds the step
to the entering variable. The basis replacement happens later. There is no
support argument, alias rejection, finite-value guard, or duplicate-basis check
inside this helper. These are relevant differences between a direct helper call
and its normal caller; do not silently strengthen its public-in-module contract.

For IEEE round-to-nearest arithmetic, old value -0, step -1 and coefficient +0
produce +0 through the original subtraction; omission preserves -0. This is an
analytic scalar example, not a Julia observation or a demonstrated reachable
solver trajectory. BigFloat also requires stored-precision and ambient-rounding
analysis, so `_is_exact` false is not an admissible omission category.
`_is_exact` true covers all Rational parameterizations; it does not by itself
establish overflow behavior, finite operands, element ownership, or helper
alias safety. No generic Rational optimization follows from this report.

## Support lifetime at the consumer

The normal entering FTRAN returns `scratch.row_solution` (lines 1259–1262).
The direction residual check reads that direction while rewriting and
invalidating `scratch.tau` and `scratch.row_rhs` (lines 827–868). Nonempty bound
flips solve into tau specifically to preserve the entering direction (lines
486–528). The final scalar updates precede pricing-weight work and factor-column
replacement (lines 1429–1438). Thus those ordinary writes alone do not destroy
row_solution support. This is a local source statement, not whole-call ownership
or observer neutrality: bound flips can publish observer events, accepted
refinement and recovery can replace direction values, and aliases require
separate analysis.

Existing support validity is owned by `scratch.hypersparse` and its six borrowed
buffers. `_pipeline_vector!` is not a read-only admission query: it can construct
or replace the workspace, rebuild invalid support by scanning all values, or
compact valid support in O(s). A valid cache may contain cancelled zero entries;
only the immediately completed canonical producer has the stronger guarantee.
Any future value consumer must tolerate those entries or qualify compaction
cost. A new full support scan solely to save an m-entry update cannot be
reported as removing that scan.

